// Additional DB-only invariants reserved precisely for Task 3.4 baseline SQL:
// - CHECK PartnerEarning.amountIdr > 0, PartnerPayout.amountIdr > 0,
//   PayoutAllocation.amountIdr > 0, and LedgerEntry.amountIdrSigned <> 0.
// - DEFERRABLE constraint trigger at transaction commit requiring every
//   LedgerTransaction to have at least two LedgerEntry rows whose signed sum is zero.
// - Append-only UPDATE/DELETE denial triggers for LedgerTransaction and LedgerEntry;
//   corrections must insert a compensating transaction with a new eventKey.
// - Constraint trigger requiring PartnerEarning.amountIdr = OrderSnapshot.payoutIdr.
// - Constraint trigger requiring each allocation to equal its whole Earning amount,
//   each PartnerPayout.amountIdr to equal SUM(PayoutAllocation.amountIdr), and allocation
//   rows to be immutable after payout leaves requested EXCEPT the one-way release
//   (set releasedAt) applied when the payout is rejected/failed, which frees the
//   Earning for a fresh payout while keeping the row for audit.
// - Partial unique index on PayoutAllocation.earningId WHERE releasedAt IS NULL:
//   an Earning may sit in at most one ACTIVE (non-rejected/failed) allocation, so a
//   rejected/failed payout no longer strands its returned-to-available Earnings.
// - CHECK/trigger requiring PAID payouts to have paidAt, processedByAdminId, the
//   bank_transfer_manual method, and a non-null paymentReference; PostgreSQL's unique
//   constraint generated by @unique permits multiple NULLs but rejects duplicate
//   non-null payment references.
// - Trigger preventing mutation of destinationSnapshotJsonEncrypted after payout
//   creation and preventing deletion of referenced PayoutDestination rows.
// - CHECK requiring resolved/dismissed ReconciliationIssue rows to have resolvedAt
//   and resolutionAuditId, OPEN rows to have neither, plus a trigger requiring the
//   resolution AuditEvent to carry the same partnerId as the issue.
